description:
The extras-preservation pattern, applied across this diff to Person, CheckItem, Checklist, Comment, Task, TaskRights, TaskList, TaskNotification, stores unknown fields in `pub extra: serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>` with `#[serde(flatten, default, skip_serializing_if = ...)]`.

Deserialize-side: serde consumes known wire-renamed fields (e.g. `@type`, `mayRSVP`, `taskListId`) into typed fields and routes the rest into `extra`. Correct round-trip for inputs.

Producer-side hazard: nothing prevents Rust code from inserting a known wire-name into `extra` directly. e.g.:

    let mut t = Task::default();
    t.at_type = Some("Task".into());
    t.extra.insert("@type".into(), json!("AlsoTask"));
    serde_json::to_string(&t).unwrap();
    // emits: {"@type":"Task","@type":"AlsoTask"}  -- duplicate key in JSON

Duplicate JSON keys are technically valid JSON; consumers picking the "first" vs "last" key get different values. RFC 8259 §4 forbids it as "SHOULD NOT". Server-side parsers vary in behavior.

This is hypothetical today (no production code does this) but is a footgun on the public API surface. The `extra` field is `pub`, so any contributor can populate it.

Fix options:
  (a) Add a debug_assert in a custom Serialize impl that rejects extras keys colliding with known wire names. Imposes a Serialize boilerplate cost per type.
  (b) Wrap `extra` in a newtype `ExtraFields(Map<String, Value>)` whose `insert()` rejects reserved keys. Trades public-field-access ergonomics for safety. Worse fit with `#[serde(flatten)]`.
  (c) Document the constraint on each `extra` field and rely on contributor discipline. Cheapest, weakest.

The workspace AGENTS.md extras-preservation section does not name this hazard. Recommend at minimum a one-line rustdoc note on each `extra` field: "Inserting a key that matches a typed field's wire name (e.g. `@type`, `mayRSVP`) will produce duplicate keys on the wire — do not do this." Decide centrally and propagate via the JMAP-lbdy epic.
